{
  "skillId": "evaluate-trig-ratio",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
"intro": "A calculator stores the value of every trigonometric ratio. Once the angle is known, a few keystrokes turn $\\sin\\theta$, $\\cos\\theta$ or $\\tan\\theta$ into a decimal, which is then rounded as the question asks.",
"facts": [
"The calculator must be in **degree** mode when entering an angle as a bare number. Entering $20$ with the sine key in radian mode gives approximately $0.9129$; the explicitly degree-labelled angle gives $\\sin 20^{\\circ}\\approx 0.3420$.",
"For an acute angle, $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cos\\theta$ lie between $0$ and $1$, so a value above $1$ signals a keying error.",
"$\\tan\\theta$ has no upper limit: $\\tan 64^{\\circ}\\approx 2.0503$ and $\\tan 82.3^{\\circ}\\approx 7.3962$.",
"In $12\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ the $12$ multiplies the ratio, so evaluate $\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ and then multiply.",
"In $\\dfrac{9}{\\sin 57^{\\circ}}$ the ratio is the divisor, so evaluate $\\sin 57^{\\circ}$ and then divide $9$ by it.",
"Round once, at the very end, because rounding the ratio first shifts the last digits of the answer."
],
"steps": [
"Check the calculator is in degree mode",
"Evaluate the trigonometric ratio",
"Complete any multiplication or division, keeping full precision",
"Round to the number of decimal places asked for"
],
"workedExamples": [
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate (a) $\\sin25^{\\circ}$, (b) $\\cos52^{\\circ}$ and (c) $\\tan38^{\\circ}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "Set the calculator to degree mode.\n\n(a) $\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sin25^{\\circ}\\\\&=0.422618\\ldots\\\\&\\approx0.4226\\end{aligned}$\n\n(b) $\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\cos52^{\\circ}\\\\&=0.615661\\ldots\\\\&\\approx0.6157\\end{aligned}$\n\n(c) $\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\tan38^{\\circ}\\\\&=0.781285\\ldots\\\\&\\approx0.7813\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $8\\cos32^{\\circ}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "In degree mode, evaluate the cosine and multiply by $8$. Keep full precision until the final rounding.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}8\\cos32^{\\circ}\\\\&=6.784384\\ldots\\\\&\\approx6.7844\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\dfrac{\\sin61^{\\circ}}{5}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "In degree mode, evaluate the sine, then divide its value by $5$.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{\\sin61^{\\circ}}{5}\\\\&=0.174923\\ldots\\\\&\\approx0.1749\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\dfrac{11}{\\sin49^{\\circ}}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "In degree mode, divide $11$ by the sine value. Keep the denominator at full precision.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{11}{\\sin49^{\\circ}}\\\\&=14.575142\\ldots\\\\&\\approx14.5751\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\dfrac{6\\cos23^{\\circ}}{7}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "In degree mode, evaluate the cosine, multiply by $6$, then divide by $7$. Round only the final result.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{6\\cos23^{\\circ}}{7}\\\\&=0.789004\\ldots\\\\&\\approx0.7890\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\dfrac{10}{4\\tan35^{\\circ}}$ correct to $4$ decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "In degree mode, evaluate the tangent and multiply by $4$ to obtain the whole denominator. Divide $10$ by that unrounded value.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{10}{4\\tan35^{\\circ}}\\\\&=\\frac{10}{2.800830\\ldots}\\\\&=3.570370\\ldots\\\\&\\approx3.5704\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  }
]
},
